{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they saw the Elohim of Israel</b> — R. Abraham ibn Ezra explained it as a prophetic vision, like \"I saw Adonai standing upon the altar\" (Amos 9:1). <b>And under His feet was like the work of a pavement of sapphire</b> — this is what Ezekiel saw: \"the likeness of a throne, in appearance like sapphire stone\" (Ezekiel 1:26). <b>And like the essence of the heavens for purity</b> — that they saw beneath the pavement of sapphire something like the essence of the heavens for purity; that is the rakia, \"like the appearance of the terrible ice stretched over the heads of the living creatures\" (Ezekiel 1:22). Now here it is written \"and they saw the Elohim of Israel,\" and there (Ezekiel 10:20) it is written \"this is the living creature that I saw under the Elohim of Israel\" — for he took a shortcut, because it is beneath the rakia, which is beneath the throne, and everything is beneath the glorious Name. And according to the plain sense, the reason for \"Elohim of Israel\" is to say that the merit of Israel their father was with them to merit them for this vision.\n\nAnd by way of truth: because He mentioned at the giving of the Torah \"and Elohim spoke\" (Exodus 19:25), and it is as He said, \"Behold, YHWH our Elohim has shown us His glory and His greatness, and His voice we have heard out of the midst of the fire\" (Deuteronomy 5:21), He explained here that they saw the Elohim of Israel — and He did not say, as He would say in every other place, 'YHWH Elohim of Israel.' And He mentioned this regarding them to say that the elders grasped in this vision more than the rest of the people, who saw on earth the great fire (Deuteronomy 4:36), for the people were in the thick cloud and darkness (Deuteronomy 5:19). And Onkelos alludes to this, for he translated \"and they saw the glory of the Elohim of Israel,\" and did not translate \"and the glory of HaShem was revealed to them,\" as is his custom in other places."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The Hebrew switches between third-person references to God and first-person divine speech; I preserved this faithfully rather than normalizing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:15:43.886684684Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}